Kim Santepheap, a spokesman for the Ministry of Justice, told the Post yesterday that what Minister Keut Rith wanted to do was to provide vocational training to prisoners who are currently serving their sentences in prisons so that they can have a career. Can do business after they are released from prison.
However, he has not yet confirmed the timing of the training, which will have to wait to discuss with the relevant institutions.
According to Kim Santepheap, the reason for wanting vocational training in this prison is to prevent those prisoners from re-integrating into society when he commits crimes while they are unable to afford a job or occupation. Other to earn a living.
The plan was presented to the United Nations in collaboration with the Ministry of Justice, the General Department of Prisons and the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, which could open a training course in prisons.
Mr. Kim Santepheap said:
"Once they have a livelihood and an income, the chances of committing a crime are non-existent and a new measure to prevent while we integrate the prisoner into society is to make him Have a proper professional career.
The President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, Mr. Sok Touch, said that vocational training for prisoners who are being held in prisons is a necessary thing to do, because if they do not do it when they come back, they will not be able to do it. Re-offenses that affect security and social order are on the rise.
Mr. Sok Touch stated that short-term prisoners should be trained in vegetable growing, and long-term prisoners should be trained in various professions so that they can have skills and be able to work.
