How do you see the effects of COVID 19 to the economy and business of Cambodia?
This is a previously ever-existed crisis that it affects the health and social health so much around the world.
In addition to affecting human health because of the 19, we think the global economy will decline.
Our estimate shows that global economic slowdown may be more serious than the economic crisis in 2008, which has led to investment, investment and trade trade.
The crisis is spreading across the most vulnerable developing countries.
While Cambodia is among some countries that have avoided the health crisis, but it is affecting the economy.
The most affected sector is tourism, exports, production and construction sectors that contribute more than 70% of economic growth, and these sectors create more than 39% of the year 2019.
It is now estimated that at least 1.76 million jobs are facing problems.
According to the World Bank report recently.
What do you think about these challenges in the present environment?
This is not a problem that affects Cambodia alone, it is global exposure.
Many companies have reduced operations and cut staff.
Especially to the MSMES, which is vulnerable to the global crisis, and if the issue continues to last, the damage will last, increasingly increasing the global economic recovery or duration Average.
Developing countries are under deteriorating commerce and financial environment.
We are seeing more stressful financial situations for developing countries because of flows of major flow and currency dropped.
In response to this situation, MSMES is working with the customer to delay the payment and find the appropriate solution to the debtor repayment from the creditor.
The IFC is working rapidly to respond to economic impact through financing that will help the company to continue operations and pay off their employees.
The IFC is financing $ 8 billion to its clients in the private sector in the world to support the economy and protect work during the unprecedented crisis.
The IFC's most financing is provided to a banking client so that they can continue to finance the capital, especially the supply chain, especially small and medium-sized enterprises .
We strongly believe that support for the private sector is important to recharge the economic stability and maintenance.
We are working with existing customer institutions around the world to provide short-term support and support operations during the recovery phase to continue to provide financials to small businesses.
As for the counseling, IFC is also working with financial institutions to strengthen the risk management and build their capacity to help SMEs to manage the crisis and give them skills and Work training to help them rebuild their business.
In our experience of economic crisis, including the global economic crisis in 2008, taught us that keeping bankruptcy is the key to helping to maintain and reduce economic damage.
To ensure a strong recovery, there is more necessary efforts to ensure macroeconomic stability and the financial environment as well as the company's business environment.
Trade diversification will reduce logistics costs and improve work skills that will increase economic strength for medium and long term.
